## 2b. THE USER-LOOKUP GUARDS ARE CLASSIFIERS — their legitimate shapes, and every READY claim as a check that CAN FAIL (BRIEF_TEMPLATE §2a applies: RD-856's pronoun guard and RD-857's whole-word list and department/location guard decide which handler answers)
**The ordinary shapes the classifier will see in service, legitimate first (each is the verdict shown, put through the helper clause by clause — drive each through the REAL route on V3, belted, a scratch driver in YOUR evidence dir, never committed):**

| shape — as a user really produces it | expected route | the clause that yields it | predicted-by |
|---|---|---|---|
| "how much has Daniel Hughes printed?" (a seeded user) | user-activity handler | 1st pattern capture "daniel hughes" passes `isValidName`; matches a user | READY |
| "how much has James Mitchell / Mei Lin / Priya Patel printed?" | user-activity handler (the base's substring test rejected them) | `isCommonWord` is whole-word | READY |
| "what did we spend on toner cartridges last quarter?" | the normal path (the stand-in), NOT the not-found text | RD-856 `NON_NAME_LEADS` first word `we` | READY |
| "what did the most active user print?" / "who has not printed this week?" / "what did our team print last week?" | the normal path (function word / negation / `team`) | commonWords whole word | READY |
| "how much has engineering printed this month?" / "what did melbourne hq print last week?" / "what did sydney print last week?" | the normal path | `captureIsDepartmentOrLocation` after a failed lookup | READY (ANSWER-10 word-level) |
| "how much has zebedee printed?" (an unknown name sharing no word with any department/location) | the UNCHANGED not-found text | the guard returns false | READY (ANSWER-10 control) |
| "what did the users / teams / departments / printers print?" (plurals) | the normal path | plural rule `s`/`es` | READY |
| "how much has Sydney Smith printed?" with NO such seeded user | **drafter's reading: the guard FIRES (the capture shares the word `sydney` with a location value) and the normal model path answers instead of "couldn't find any users matching "sydney smith""** — ANSWER-10 calls this acceptable (*"an UNKNOWN name sharing a word with a department or location gets the normal model answer instead of the not-found text, which is acceptable"*) | the guard's `cw.every(w ∈ vw)` / `vw.every(w ∈ cw)` | drafter (READ-ONLY-INFERRED; r6 measures it) |
| "how much has IT printed?" / "what did HR print?" (the seed has `it`, `hr` as department words; `it` is also a pronoun) | **drafter's reading: with RD-856 the capture "it" is a pronoun lead and is rejected as a name before the lookup; "hr" reaches the guard and fires** — say which route each takes | `NON_NAME_LEADS` vs the guard | drafter (READ-ONLY-INFERRED) |
| a real seeded user who shares a word with a department/location (the seed has none — add one in a scratch seed) | the user-activity handler (the guard runs only after the lookup failed) | the guard's position inside `matchingUsers.length === 0` | Tuesday ANSWER-10 |
| a hostile capture: a 31-character name, `__proto__`, a name with an apostrophe (`o'brien`), mixed case, a trailing `s` on a real surname (`Hughes`, `Davis`, `Jones`) | no exception, no prototype hit; surnames ending in `s`/`es` are NOT stripped into a skip word unless the stripped form is an entry | `isCommonWord`'s `endsWith('s'/'es')` | drafter (READ-ONLY-INFERRED) |

**Each READY claim → a check that CAN FAIL, positive and negative controls:** **C1** "RD-856: 22 pronoun/determiner leads, none a real first name" — test every lead against a name list of your own and against the seed (a lead that is also a seeded first name is a REGRESSION). **C2** "RD-857: no answer text changes" — diff every user-visible string the lookup can emit between `f4026fba` and `1da0bd46` (`git diff -U0`, strings only): the not-found sentence and the handler's lines unchanged. **C3** "RD-857: the guard needs no new data path" — the helper's inputs are `allLogs` already in hand (read it); a `logs` that is not an array returns false (a planted `undefined`). **C4** "RD-857: the email branch and the multiple-match branch are untouched" — `git diff` shows no hunk there. **C5** "the guard fires only when no user matched" — a planted two-user match does not reach it. **C6** "`break userLookup` falls through to the normal analyzeQuery path immediately after" — read the code after the labelled block; drive "engineering" and show the stand-in answered. **C7** "RD-857: 15 cells, the sydney/melbourne/engineering cells discriminate" — r6's M857-b/e. Controls: every cell's NEGATIVE (the cell goes green when the behaviour is present) and POSITIVE (red when absent) read in r5/r6.

